 about the team right yeah of  course you can't do it yourself  you're married to a chef you know you  know that it takes a brigade  it takes a brigade right of people and  so that's how you end up doing it our  motto and our company is delicious  to serve with graciousness every day  right get up and show up and be present  and so that's what we do and that's what  comes across in our restaurant so  whether it's the humble pizzeria or it's  the fanciest of that we have like the  Dahlia lounge so that's that's what we  try to do this one dressing because you  were self-taught you had no formal  culinary training so first well let's  start where did your love of food come  from well grandma's you know that's kind  of a classic scenario though but I I  remember clearly coming down into the  kitchen when grandma was visiting and  she would have the German schnecken or a  cinnamon roll pull apart kind of rolled  out on the kitchen table on towels right  and then I just begged to help and she'd  let me spread the butter or sprinkle the  cinnamon sugar and then we'd roll him up  with that good toasty warm yeast dough  and that smell to this day when I make  snakin I can really you know  visualize and have the whole process in  my head and then of course from there my  interest was piqued went to home at  class in high school mainly to meet  girls turned out good but at the same  time I was the only guy and you know in  the moment very awesome and I really  kind of fell for it not only the girls  but the cooking right I fell for it  enough that I would bring the food from  class to my football practice is a very  bad idea because the first thing we made  was cream puffs right is a classic cream  puffs on the football field I think what  my nickname was not good how did you  develop the confidence to open your own  restaurant I mean and in the self-taught  you know vein that we were talking about  how did you teach yourself well nobody's  ever really self-taught in my mind 